---
source: crates/wolf_sema/tests/attr_diagnostics.rs
expression: "render_attrs(\"#[repr(packed)]\\nstruct A {\\n    x: u8,\\n}\\n\\n\\\n             #[repr(c, packed, align(8))]\\nstruct B {\\n    x: u8,\\n}\\n\\n\\\n             #[repr(c, packed)]\\nstruct C[T] {\\n    x: T,\\n}\\n\\n\\\n             #[repr(c, c)]\\nstruct D {\\n    x: u8,\\n}\\n\\n\\\n             #[repr(align(8))]\\nstruct E {\\n    x: u8,\\n}\\n\")"
---
error[E0820]: `packed` modifies the C layout, so it is spelled beside `c`
 --> mem:///main.lu:1:8
  |
1 | #[repr(packed)]
  |        ^^^^^^ this representation cannot be laid out
  |
  = note: write `#[repr(c, packed)]`: the layout `packed` changes is the C one ([abi.layout.c]); a
    native-layout struct has no fixed offsets to pack or align.

error[E0820]: a struct is packed or aligned, not both
 --> mem:///main.lu:6:8
  |
6 | #[repr(c, packed, align(8))]
  |        ^ this representation cannot be laid out
  |
  = note: `packed` is alignment 1 and `align(N)` is alignment N; one struct cannot be both
    ([abi.layout.packed], [abi.layout.align]). Pack the struct, or align it.

error[E0820]: a generic struct cannot be packed or aligned
  --> mem:///main.lu:11:8
   |
11 | #[repr(c, packed)]
   |        ^ this representation cannot be laid out
   |
   = note: its layout would change with each instantiation's field types, so no query could answer
     it ([abi.layout.query]); declare a concrete struct.

error[E0820]: `c` is named twice in this struct's representation
  --> mem:///main.lu:16:8
   |
16 | #[repr(c, c)]
   |        ^ this representation cannot be laid out
   |
   = note: each representation is named once ([abi.layout.c])

error[E0820]: `align(N)` modifies the C layout, so it is spelled beside `c`
  --> mem:///main.lu:21:8
   |
21 | #[repr(align(8))]
   |        ^^^^^^^^ this representation cannot be laid out
   |
   = note: write `#[repr(c, align(N))]`: the layout `align(N)` changes is the C one
     ([abi.layout.c]); a native-layout struct has no fixed offsets to pack or align.
